WHERE IT COMES FROM

picowallet, by Austin Griffith (https://github.com/austintgriffith/picowallet), MIT,
Copyright (c) 2026 Austin Griffith.
  picowallet showed that a Raspberry Pi Pico can drive an ATECC608 from MicroPython, rebuild an
  EIP-712 digest on the device, show it, and sign with a key that never leaves the chip. This
  signer was ported from a MicroPython one built on picowallet's code. These parts go back to
  files that started in picowallet:
    core/src/atecc.rs           from atecc.py: the ATECC608's packets and wake sequence
    core/src/blockies.rs        from blockies.py
    firmware/src/bin/main.rs    the screen's start-up sequence, from lcd.py
  They are under the MIT license in LICENSE, which carries Austin Griffith's copyright.

OTHER WORK THIS USES

Atkinson Hyperlegible Next and Atkinson Hyperlegible Mono (Braille Institute of America,
  https://github.com/googlefonts/atkinson-hyperlegible-next), SIL Open Font License 1.1: the
  glyphs in fonts/qf_*.bin are rendered from them. The license is in fonts/OFL.txt.

Blockies (https://github.com/ethereum/blockies): core/src/blockies.rs draws the same identicons,
  ported from its algorithm.

Rust crates, each under its own license (MIT or Apache-2.0 for the ones named here): esp-hal,
  esp-bootloader-esp-idf, esp-backtrace, esp-println and their dependencies in the firmware;
  sha3 in core. Cargo.lock in core/ and firmware/ lists every one.

esptool-js 0.7.0 (Espressif, https://github.com/espressif/esptool-js), Apache-2.0: the flasher
  web/ loads, unchanged, in web/vendor/esptool-js/ with its license and checksums (SOURCE.md).

The test vectors in vectors/ are described in vectors/SOURCE.md.
